export { traditionHTML } from './heritage.mjs?v=13';
export const rulesHTML = `<h2 id="dialog-title">The essentials</h2><p>Collect more shells than the other player. You each have a row of seven pits, with four shells in every pit.</p><button class="primary-button" data-guide="tutorial">Learn by playing</button><ol class="essential-rules"><li><strong>Pick up.</strong> Choose any occupied pit in your own row and lift all its shells.</li><li><strong>Sow.</strong> Place one shell in each next pit: right along the bottom row, then left along the top. Skip the two large end bowls.</li><li><strong>Continue.</strong> When your hand is empty, look at the next pit. If it has shells, pick them up and keep sowing.</li><li><strong>Collect.</strong> If that next pit is empty, collect the shells from the pit just beyond it. Your turn ends, even if there is nothing to collect.</li></ol><p>Tap a wooden pit to start. Automatic places each handful for you; with <strong>Tap next pickup</strong> on, tap the glowing pit when your hand empties. Turn it off for a continuous turn. Hands-on lets you place each shell by tapping or sliding along the pits. Your side is the left column when the board is upright. In <strong>Settings → Playing preferences</strong>, try Hands-on or Automatic sowing and choose a comfortable speed.</p><details class="guide-details"><summary>Finishing a round</summary><p>The round ends when the next player has no occupied pit. Each player collects the shells left in their own row. Most shells wins; equal scores draw.</p><p>To finish a game that keeps repeating, this app also settles the board when the same position appears three times or a relay loops. A new round starts with four shells in every pit.</p></details><details class="guide-details"><summary>Playing your way</summary><p>Play solo against the Bot on Easy, Medium or Hard, or take turns with a friend on the same screen. Settings lets you choose hands-on or automatic sowing, animation speed, and shell sounds. Hint suggests a pit; Undo takes back your last turn and the bot’s reply.</p><p>Families play different versions. This one uses next-pit pickup and capture beyond the gap. It does not capture opposite pits or automatically collect groups of four.</p></details>`;
export const aboutHTML = `<h2 id="dialog-title">A game kept in play.</h2><p>Chenne Mane is a traditional game of coastal Karnataka, learned around wooden boards with shells or seeds.</p><p>This app shares one coastal family version. Households have their own ways of playing; the short guide explains the version here.</p><div class="dialog-actions"><button class="primary-button" data-guide="tutorial">Learn by playing</button><button class="text-button" data-guide="tradition">Our Tulunadu tribute</button></div><details class="guide-details"><summary>About this version</summary><p>The rules draw on family accounts by <a href="https://soumya-alteridem.blogspot.com/2011/07/chennemane.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Soumya</a> and <a href="https://havyakavishwa.com/banni-chennemaneli-ondu-aata-aduvo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pallavi Shyam</a>. The app plays a single round, with a finish for repeating positions.</p></details>`;
